(1881441, 254445, 41066, '<td valign="top">I think this photograph has a lot of potential.  I would adjust the contrast on it as well as the curves to really make the colors "pop".  I think it has the makings of being a really magical photo.  You might also try using a bit of dodging and burning on the brick walls and the bridge to help them stand out more too. I love the off centre composition.</td>', 'I think this photograph has a lot of potential.  I would adjust the contrast on it as well as the curves to really make the colors "pop".  I think it has the makings of being a really magical photo.  You might also try using a bit of dodging and burning on the brick walls and the bridge to help them stand out more too. I love the off centre composition.', '2005-11-18 21:54:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(1881751, 255400, 19708, '<td valign="top">Can''t thank you all enough for the gracious comments here.<br/>Pictures do not do the moment justice and should be experienced at least once.</td>', 'Can''t thank you all enough for the gracious comments here.Pictures do not do the moment justice and should be experienced at least once.', '2005-11-18 23:20:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(1882451, 255284, 36060, '<td valign="top">The reason why my photo Cold mountain was disqualified is that I removed two small spots (I am told because of dust on the sensor) from the image. These spots have been a problem on my photos lately, but removing them does not change the photos a bit, so I thought I was not doing anything wrong by doing so in this case. It was not my intention to cheat or do something that is not  permitted.<br/>	It is of course necessary to have rules in competitions like here on DPC, but it is my opinion that rules that disqualify photos on these grounds are not about good photography, but rather a stupid reason to get rid of good photos.<br/>	In all my challenges I have tried to do my best to follow the rules and also in this challenge, so I am not reconciled to this conclusion. In fact it did not cross my mind that I was doing something wrong by taking out these two little spots and was very surprised when the photo was disqualified. <br/>	Because of this experience I have had and probably many others I would suggest that these rules were changed. It would be nice to have some discussions about this matter. <br/></td>', 'The reason why my photo Cold mountain was disqualified is that I removed two small spots (I am told because of dust on the sensor) from the image. These spots have been a problem on my photos lately, but removing them does not change the photos a bit, so I thought I was not doing anything wrong by doing so in this case. It was not my intention to cheat or do something that is not  permitted.	It is of course necessary to have rules in competitions like here on DPC, but it is my opinion that rules that disqualify photos on these grounds are not about good photography, but rather a stupid reason to get rid of good photos.	In all my challenges I have tried to do my best to follow the rules and also in this challenge, so I am not reconciled to this conclusion. In fact it did not cross my mind that I was doing something wrong by taking out these two little spots and was very surprised when the photo was disqualified. 	Because of this experience I have had and probably many others I would suggest that these rules were changed. It would be nice to have some discussions about this matter. ', '2005-11-19 08:19:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(1882597, 252970, 46920, '<td valign="top">Yes, I know...those powerlines.  They take away from the beauty of nature.  Being new to all of this, I was wondering if anyone knew the best way to edit the lines out of the photo.  Which is the best program to use and how do you go about it?  Any advice would be very helpful and appreciated.  Thanks.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-11-19 10:48:50.</i></td>', 'Yes, I know...those powerlines.  They take away from the beauty of nature.  Being new to all of this, I was wondering if anyone knew the best way to edit the lines out of the photo.  Which is the best program to use and how do you go about it?  Any advice would be very helpful and appreciated.  Thanks.', '2005-11-19 10:47:50.000000', '2005-11-19 10:48:50.000000', 0),
(1882742, 253342, 32818, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Tati:</b></div><hr/><i> we speak english only here.thanx. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Too bad to judge just because of the title in french! I won''t do that again cause I can see that it wasn''t well accepted.. BUT.. I am french and I speak perfectly english? Can you see my point? Thanks for the comments all! Very appreciated!!!!!!</td>', 'Originally posted by Tati: we speak english only here.thanx.  Too bad to judge just because of the title in french! I won''t do that again cause I can see that it wasn''t well accepted.. BUT.. I am french and I speak perfectly english? Can you see my point? Thanks for the comments all! Very appreciated!!!!!!', '2005-11-19 12:01:37.000000', NULL, 0),
